    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/assistant-professor-of-occupational-therapy-pediatrics/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Occupational Therapy (Pediatrics)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of OT (Pediatrics) Job Listing | Presbyterian College Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\r\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Assistant Professor of Assistant Professor of Occupational Therapy (Pediatrics) Assistant Professor of Occupational Therapy (Pediatrics) About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Assistant Professor of Occupational Therapy Assistant Professor of Occupational Therapy (Pediatrics) Position Description This is a full-time, twelve-month, non-tenure-track instructional faculty position that reports to the Program Director of the Occupational Therapy Doctoral Program in the School of Health Professions at Presbyterian College. This position will contribute the education, mentorship, and professional development of entry-level doctoral occupational therapy students.  The faculty member will provide primary instructional expertise in pediatric occupational therapy while also contributing to curricular, experiential, and professional development needs of the program. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. The duties of the position are as follows: Pediatric Teaching Responsibilities Develop, coordinate, and teach pediatric occupational therapy content across the OTD curriculum, including pediatric evaluation, assessment, intervention, clinical reasoning, documentation, and evidence-based practice. Provide instruction addressing occupational performance, participation, and development across infancy, childhood, and adolescence, with consideration of physical, cognitive, psychosocial, sensory, developmental, and environmental factors. Integrate pediatric content across occupational therapy practice settings, including early intervention, schools, outpatient pediatrics, inpatient and rehabilitation settings, community-based programs, and other emerging areas of pediatric practice. Design and facilitate engaging classroom, laboratory, simulation, case-based, and experiential learning activities that promote clinical reasoning and application of occupational therapy theory to pediatric practice. Incorporate evidence-based pediatric assessment tools, intervention approaches, clinical documentation, outcome measures, and current professional standards into coursework. Teach students to analyze the relationship between child development, occupations, routines, environments, and participation in meaningful activities. Incorporate family-centered, occupation-centered, culturally responsive, trauma-informed, and developmentally appropriate approaches to pediatric occupational therapy practice. Prepare students to collaborate effectively with parents, caregivers, educators, healthcare providers, and other professionals involved in the care and support of children and families. Incorporate community-based pediatric learning experiences that allow students to apply classroom knowledge to authentic occupational therapy situations. This expectation aligns with the OTD program’s emphasis on experiential learning, community engagement, and service. Evaluate student learning through appropriately designed examinations, practical examinations, assignments, laboratory performance, presentations, clinical reasoning activities, and other assessment methods. Teaching Responsibilities Teach a course load appropriate to the discipline and position. Plan and organize instruction to maximize student learning objectives. Implement contemporary teaching and learning strategies to communicate subject matter to students. Modify, as needed, instructional approaches to address student diversity. Learn and use technology to enhance teaching and the educational experience. i.e. the internet, Institutional learning system (Blackboard etc.), distance coursework (tele-medicine /video conferencing as needed), and other interactive technology, as appropriate. Encourage and facilitate the development of communication skills and higher order thinking skills through appropriate assignments. Contribute to the selection and development of instructional materials in accordance with course objectives and the curricular design. Incorporate specific core competencies into specific coursework taught. Develop, update, and post course syllabi in a timely manner in accordance with program policies. Managing classrooms and labs to ensure equipment is maintained and up-to-date. Serve as a mentor and advisor for student’s doctoral projects as assigned. Teaching Outcomes Develop and explain methods that fairly measure student progress toward student learning Outcomes and specific course objectives. Evaluate student performance fairly and consistently and return student work promptly to promote maximum learning. Identify students at risk for not meeting performance criteria in order to assist student with engaging the appropriate resources for success. Maintain accurate documentation/records of student progress and submit final grade rosters to Program Director/Administrative staff each semester according to established deadlines. Meet with advisees at least once a semester and maintain accurate documentation any issues, progress, or positives discussed. (note: documentation to be placed in student’s program file) Support of Program Policies and Procedures Post and maintain regular office hours to ensure accessibility for advisement and consultation. Serve as faculty advisor within the current program/college advisement system as assigned. Maintain confidentiality of student information. (i.e. FERPA, HIPPA, ETC) Substitute for other instructors within field or discipline in case of an absence. Exercise stewardship (fiduciary responsibility) of college facilities and materials. Participate in peer evaluation of faculty including observations and reviews as required. Recruit students by supporting and participating in official college recruiting functions, visitation, and hosting of visiting groups as well as participation in interviews, vetting, and decision making of potential admissions to the program. (Student Affairs) Participation in the continuous quality improvement of the OTD program including course/programmatic changes. (Academic Affairs) Facilitate and participate in connections with programs (community, medical, and other organizations) to enhance the OTD program. Participation as a committee member with responsibilities as assigned for national programmatic accreditation. Representing the College/Department in community events relative to the OTD Program. Assisting advisees with registration in the Fall, Spring and Summer as needed. Service Serves on programmatic and university committees as assigned. Actively participates in his/her professional association(s). Serves as a university liaison in community and/or professional activities. Can use 4-8 hours/week to engage in professional development (clinical work, research, etc.) as approved by the program director. College and Program Activities Serve on college and program committees as assigned. Participate in meeting and events required by the college and program administrators. Respond in a timely fashion to information requests from college and program administrators. Support both part-time and full-time colleagues. Contribute to program and college curriculum development processes. Participate in graduation ceremonies Demonstrated strong interpersonal skills in communication with students, colleagues, staff and administrators as an individual or as a part of a team. Maintain familiarity with program and college goals, mission, and long-range plans. Contribute to planning and development processes through appropriate systems. Participate in professional activities that contribute to the educational goals of the department college, and its constituents. Perform professional responsibilities in accordance with pertinent goals, missions, and plans of the college and programs. Scholarship Establishes and implements an approved scholarship plan/faculty development plan as required by accreditation and PC standards. Stay current with clinical practice and evidence that support content area expertise, professional growth, and professional certification and licensure requirements. Disseminates scholarly work consistent with PC policies and accreditation expectations. Demonstrates commitment to scholarly advancement at state, national, and international professional events (minimum one per year). Professional Behaviors Serve as a role model for faculty members, students, and staff in the department and college. Stay current in subject matter through professional development, involvement in professional organizations, and or attending professional meetings, conferences, or workshops. Demonstrate sensitivity to student needs and circumstances. Administrative As stated in teaching requirements for program coursework. No authority or responsibility for the supervision of others, project direction, or program administration unless assigned by the program chair. Other Other duties as assigned by the Program Director. Reporting Relationships Direction Received: Receives guidance and direct supervision from the Program Director/Chair. Minimum Requirements 3-5 years of experience as an Occupational Therapist in a clinical practice setting. Academic and experiential qualifications and backgrounds (identified in documented descriptions of roles and responsibilities) that are necessary to meet program objectives and the mission of the institution. Expertise in a specific area of the discipline Doctoral Degree required in Occupational Therapy or related field of study. National Board for Certification in Occupational Therapy State Licensure Preferred Requirements Teaching experience Student interaction as clinical instructor or coordinator To Apply Interested individuals should provide a cover letter explaining interest in the position, curriculum vitae, unofficial transcripts from all graduate institutions, and three references with contact information. Materials should be sent electronically to the Program Director, Dr. Moni Keen, at [email protected] . Please contact Dr. Moni Keen , Program Director of the OTD Program, if you have any questions about the position. About Presbyterian College Presbyterian College (PC) is a liberal arts college affiliated with the Presbyterian Church USA. The college is committed to an equitable and inclusive learning environment for all students, supports academic freedom, and encourages community involvement. Located in Clinton, South Carolina, PC is approximately 40 minutes from Greenville and Spartanburg, South Carolina and about one hour from Columbia, South Carolina. The region offers abundant opportunity for social and recreational activities, with easy access to Asheville and Charlotte, North Carolina, and Atlanta, Georgia. Presbyterian College is an EEO employer and does not discriminate against any person or group on the basis of age, color, disability, gender, pregnancy, national origin, race, religion, sexual orientation, veteran status or genetic information.  PC is building a culturally diverse faculty and staff committed to working in a multicultural environment and encourages applications from minorities and women. Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources OTD Program Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at [email protected] for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community"
    },
    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/assistant-professor-of-pharmacy-practice/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Pharmacy Practice",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Pharmacy Practice | Presbyterian College Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\r\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Assistant Professor of Assistant Professor of Pharmacy Practice Assistant Professor of Pharmacy Practice About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Assistant Professor of Pharmacy Practice Assistant Professor of Pharmacy Practice Position Overview Presbyterian College School of Pharmacy is seeking qualified individuals for a full-time, 12-month, non-tenure-track faculty position in Pharmacy Practice. The School is seeking a clinical specialist with experience in inpatient clinical or outpatient ambulatory practice. The successful candidate will join the School of Pharmacy as an Assistant Professor of Pharmacy Practice. The faculty member will possess a PharmD degree with successful completion of a PGY1 or PGY2 accredited residency program, or equivalent experience.  The position will report to the Chair of the Department of Pharmacy Practice. Candidates must be eligible for pharmacist licensure in the state of South Carolina. Primary Position Responsibilities Assist in the development of educational materials for laboratories and didactic courses that support the mission of the school and reflect the COEPA and ACPE standards of accreditation. Teach areas within the pharmacotherapeutics courses Teach in other areas within the curriculum Develop a program of scholarship Serve as an active member of committees assigned by the Executive Committee Serve as a clinical pharmacist either at an outpatient clinic or inpatient acute care facility for two days per week in specialty area Precept students on pharmacy practice experiences Strong communication, interpersonal, and team building skills are required Commitment to teaching excellence and encouragement of student engagement in the learning process are expected The mission of the School of Pharmacy is to provide a quality education that enables delivery of optimal team-based patient care, fosters leadership, and creates a culture of service. Our core values include quality education, commitment to scholarship, integrity, and teamwork. Our program boasts the top first-time NAPLEX (board) and MPJE (law) in the state, and has been recognized for making a significant impact in our community. Our faculty and students strive to live by our motto: “While we live, we serve.” The campus in Clinton, South Carolina is located forty-five minutes south of Greenville or Spartanburg, sixty minutes north of Columbia, and three hours from Charleston. The College offers a competitive benefits package including options for health, dental, vision, and life insurance. To Apply Review of applications will immediately. Compensation is commensurate with qualifications and experience. To apply for the position, send via e-mail a letter of intent, curriculum vitae, and contact information of three references, preferably from current or former supervisors to: Mrs. Lynn Thompson Senior Administrative Assistant to the Dean Presbyterian College School of Pharmacy Phone : 864-938-3872 Email : [email protected] Presbyterian College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The College seeks to hire the most qualified candidate and does not discriminate against any legally protected class Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources School of Pharmacy Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at [email protected] for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community"
    },
    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/assistant-professor-of-political-science/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Political Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Political Science | Presbyterian College Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\r\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Assistant Professor of Assistant Professor of Political Science Assistant Professor of Political Science About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Assistant Professor of Political Science Assistant Professor of Political Science U.S. Politics / Pre-Law Studies (Tenure-Track) Position Description The Department of Political Science at Presbyterian College invites applications for a 9-month, tenure-track, assistant professor position in U.S. politics. (The Ph.D. in political science is strongly preferred, but ABD will be considered at the rank of instructor.) The successful candidate will also serve as Director of Pre-law Studies, with a start date of August 16, 2027. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The Department of Political Science is one of the largest on campus, as it typically graduates around 15-20 students each year. External observers highlight small class sizes, a well-developed curriculum, and a faculty committed to student learning and success. In that context, successful applicants should display a strong enthusiasm for teaching and a commitment to both service and scholarship. Responsibilities Responsibilities for this position include teaching in the Pre-Law minor, including two courses in constitutional law (governmental powers, and civil rights/liberties) as well as a course in legal studies. The ideal candidate will also teach an introductory course in U.S. government as well as upper-level courses in U.S. politics or public policy, with an open field of specialization. The teaching load is seven courses per academic year (4:3). In addition to teaching, responsibilities as Director of Pre-Law Studies include coordination of the College’s established pre-law program, advising Pre-Law minors and other students interested in law, and networking with law schools and other graduate programs, including opportunities in political science, public administration, or state and local politics. The ideal candidate will also contribute to student-focused activities, including the Pre-Law and Political Science clubs, South Carolina Student Legislature, and various on-campus events. To Apply Submission of application materials by electronic mail is strongly encouraged. Interested individuals should provide, at a minimum, a letter stating interest in the position, a curriculum vitae, transcripts from undergraduate and graduate institutions, and three letters of recommendation. Materials should be sent to the Office of the Provost at [email protected] . For individual questions, please contact Dr. David Liu, chair of the Department of Political Science, at [email protected] . About Presbyterian College Presbyterian College (PC) is a liberal arts college affiliated with the Presbyterian Church USA. The college is committed to an equitable and inclusive learning environment for all students, supports academic freedom, and encourages community involvement. Located in Clinton, South Carolina, PC is approximately 40 minutes from Greenville and Spartanburg, South Carolina and about one hour from Columbia, South Carolina. The region offers abundant opportunity for social and recreational activities, with easy access to Asheville and Charlotte, North Carolina, and Atlanta, Georgia. Presbyterian College is an EEO employer and does not discriminate against any person or group on the basis of age, color, disability, gender, pregnancy, national origin, race, religion, sexual orientation, veteran status or genetic information.  PC is building a culturally diverse faculty and staff committed to working in a multicultural environment and encourages applications from minorities and women. Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at [email protected] for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community"
