    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/assistant-professor-of-psychology/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Psychology | Presbyterian College Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\r\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Assistant Professor of Assistant Professor of Psychology Assistant Professor of Psychology About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Assistant Professor of Psychology Assistant Professor of Psychology Position Description Presbyterian College invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Psychology specializing in Social Psychology , beginning in Fall 2027. We seek a broadly trained social psychologist who is committed to excellence in undergraduate teaching and who will contribute actively to the intellectual life of our department and college. A doctoral degree is required; ABD candidates will be considered. The successful candidate will teach Introduction to Psychology, Research Methods and Statistics, Social Psychology, and an upper-level course within their area of expertise. The position carries a 4-3 teaching load . The Department and College support faculty scholarship, particularly research that involves undergraduate students. Faculty are eligible to apply for internal grants supporting research activities, including conference travel. They may work with eligible students to conduct research during the summer through the College’s prestigious Summer Fellows program . We welcome candidates whose scholarship complements the department’s existing strengths and who are eager to involve undergraduate students in meaningful research experiences. The Psychology Department The Psychology Department currently consists of five full-time faculty members and offers a rigorous undergraduate program in psychological science. The psychology major includes a five-course required core in methodology and systems , a five-course foundational grouping representing major areas within psychological science—including developmental, clinical, social, cognitive, and biological—and a variety of elective courses. Approximately 25–30 psychology majors graduate each year . Additional information about major requirements and course offerings is available on the Psychology Department website . We are particularly interested in candidates who value close mentorship of undergraduate students and who will contribute to the department’s collaborative and student-centered environment. To Apply Interested candidates should submit the following materials electronically in a single PDF: A letter describing your interest in the position Curriculum vitae Statement of teaching philosophy College and graduate school transcripts Three letters of recommendation Application materials should be submitted by email to [email protected] . Questions regarding this listing may be directed to Dr. Sarah Burns, chair of the Department of Psychology, at [email protected] . Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. About Presbyterian College Presbyterian College (PC) is a liberal arts college affiliated with the Presbyterian Church USA. The college is committed to an equitable and inclusive learning environment for all students, supports academic freedom, and encourages community involvement. Located in Clinton, South Carolina, PC is approximately 40 minutes from Greenville and Spartanburg, and about one hour from Columbia. The region offers abundant opportunities for social and recreational activities, with easy access to Asheville and Charlotte, North Carolina, and Atlanta, Georgia. Presbyterian College is an EEO employer and does not discriminate against any person or group on the basis of age, color, disability, gender, pregnancy, national origin, race, religion, sexual orientation, veteran status or genetic information.  PC is building a culturally diverse faculty and staff committed to working in a multicultural environment and encourages applications from minorities and women. Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at [email protected] for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community"
    },
    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/assistant-professor-of-art/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Art (Studio Art)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Art | Presbyterian College Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\r\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Assistant Professor of Assistant Professor of Art (Studio Art) Assistant Professor of Art (Studio Art) About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Assistant Professor of Art Assistant Professor of Art (Studio Art) Position Description The Department of Visual and Performing Arts at Presbyterian College invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Art, with an emphasis in studio art, beginning Fall 2027. The ideal candidate will be a dedicated teacher who enjoys working closely with undergraduate students and contributing to the department and College community. We seek a versatile artist and teacher who can contribute broadly to our undergraduate Art program. Applicants from a wide range of studio disciplines are encouraged to apply, including drawing, painting, sculpture, ceramics, printmaking, mixed media, installation, interdisciplinary practice, and other traditional or contemporary areas. Teaching may include foundational courses such as Drawing, 2D Design, and 3D Design, as well as beginning through advanced courses related to the candidate’s area of expertise. Other responsibilities may include developing Fine Art electives, teaching Junior and Senior Seminar, advising Art majors and minors, and mentoring seniors as they prepare their Senior Exhibition. Because we are a small program, our faculty work closely with students at every level, from non-majors taking their first studio course to seniors developing independent bodies of work. We are looking for someone who values that kind of interaction and is interested in helping shape the continued growth of the Art program. The successful candidate will also maintain an active professional creative practice, contribute to departmental and College service, and be committed to teaching, advising, and mentoring undergraduate students. Minimum Requirements Required qualifications for the position include an MFA or other appropriate terminal degree in Studio Art, Fine Art, or a closely related discipline by the time of appointment. Candidates should have expertise in at least one area of studio art, along with the versatility to teach across more than one area and at different levels of the undergraduate curriculum. Preferred Qualifications Preferred qualifications include college-level teaching experience, preferably as instructor of record, and the ability to teach in more than one area of studio art. Candidates should have experience working across traditional and contemporary approaches to artmaking and mentoring advanced undergraduate students in independent studio work, professional practices, and exhibition preparation. Experience managing studio facilities and equipment is also preferred, along with an active exhibition record and continued professional development as a practicing artist. To Apply Interested individuals should electronically send the following materials to Provost Dr. Erin McAdams at [email protected] : a cover letter that describes your motivation for applying for this position curriculum vitae statement of teaching philosophy artist statement three letters of recommendation official (or unofficial) college and graduate transcripts link to professional website (if available) portfolio of 10-15 images (including titles, media, and dates) 10-15 images of student work For specific questions regarding the position, please contact Miriam Ragland, chair of the Visual and Performing Arts Department, at [email protected] . Review of applications will begin November 1, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. About Presbyterian College Presbyterian College (PC) is a liberal arts college affiliated with the Presbyterian Church USA. The college is committed to an equitable and inclusive learning environment for all students, supports academic freedom, and encourages community involvement. Located in Clinton, South Carolina, PC is approximately 40 minutes from Greenville and Spartanburg, and about one hour from Columbia. The region offers abundant opportunities for social and recreational activities, with easy access to Asheville and Charlotte, North Carolina, and Atlanta, Georgia. Presbyterian College is an EEO employer and does not discriminate against any person or group on the basis of age, color, disability, gender, pregnancy, national origin, race, religion, sexual orientation, veteran status or genetic information.  PC is building a culturally diverse faculty and staff committed to working in a multicultural environment and encourages applications from minorities and women. Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at [email protected] for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community"
    },
    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/administrative-assistant-business-office/": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Student Involvement for Recreation",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant Business Office | Presbyterian College Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\r\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Administrative Assistant to the Administrative Assistant to the Business Office Administrative Assistant to the Business Office About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Administrative Assistant Business Office Assistant Director of Student Involvement for Recreation Administrative Assistant to the Business Office Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources Position Overview The Administrative Assistant reports to the Business Office Manager and performs administrative assistant duties for the Business Office. The position also serves as the College’s Cashier and is responsible for receiving, processing, recording, and depositing payments and receipts in accordance with College procedures. Duties Distribute mail to the Business Office; open all “PC” mail and distribute. Open mail for the VPFA and Controller; date and place in designated folders for their review. Manage paper flow for the VPFA from other areas on campus, such as invoices and contracts that need VPFA signature. Maintain contract files. Assist in risk management by reviewing certificates of liability insurance and keeping them current. Complete insurance renewals. Provide workers’ compensation and general liability audit support. Compile conflict of interest forms annually. File accident claims and coordinate repairs when needed. Work with Campus Police to maintain a list of campus-owned vehicles. Coordinate paperwork when a vehicle is purchased or sold, including tax exemption forms. Maintain property files. Manage office supplies for the Business Office, including paper and coffee supplies. Manage communication between Board of Trustees committees supported by the VPFA and maintain documentation for each committee. Take minutes at Board of Trustees meetings. Receive checks, cash, and money orders for tuition in person and by mail; write receipts, prepare deposits, and post to student accounts in Banner. Receive checks, cash, and money orders for general receipts, by postal mail and by interoffice mail, including athletic game revenue; write receipts, prepare deposits, and post to the general ledger in Banner. Process credit card payments for Escrow Deposits and post to student accounts. Process credit card payments for miscellaneous general receipts and post to general ledger accounts. Check Synovus Bank website daily for incoming ACH and wire transfer credits related to student accounts; notify appropriate departments as needed, and post payments to the appropriate student account in Banner. Maintain the $900 petty cash box. Provide backup support for billing and payment questions for students and parents in person, by phone, or by mail. Set up new Laurens County Academy of Music students in Banner, manage the spreadsheet for LAC, and post payments Apply student payments from collection agency. Scan weekly checks, direct deposits, and journal entries into DocuPhase. Enter journal entries provided by the other accounting staff. Process ACH enrollments for vendors. Make weekly cash deposits at the College’s bank. Serve as the Retention Officer for the Business Office. Other duties as assigned. Qualifications Good organizational, time management, and decision-making skills are essential for this position. Ability to effectively communicate with people at all levels is also needed. Experience with Microsoft Word and Excel is a requirement. Ability to handle sensitive matters confidentially. To Apply Send a cover letter, résumé and list of three references including contact information to Presbyterian College Office of Human Resources, 503 S. Broad St., Clinton, SC 29325 or email materials to [email protected] Presbyterian College is an Equal Opportunity Employer, committed to creating and sustaining an environment in which all individuals are treated with respect and dignity. The College does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, creed, national or ethnic origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, disability, veteran status, or any other legally protected status. Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at [email protected] for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community"
    },
    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/capstone-doctoral-experience-dce-coordinator-of-occupational-therapy/": {
      "title": "Occupational Therapy Capstone / DCE Coordinator",
      "description": "Capstone Doctoral Experience (DCE) Coordinator of Occupational Therapy | Presbyterian College Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\r\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Occupational Therapy Occupational Therapy Capstone / DCE Coordinator Occupational Therapy Capstone / DCE Coordinator About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Capstone Doctoral Experience (DCE) Coordinator of Occupational Therapy Occupational Therapy Capstone / DCE Coordinator Position Description This is a full-time, 12-month non-tenure-track instructional faculty position with at least 40% designated administrative time for management and administration of the Doctoral Capstone; this position reports to the Program Director of the Occupational Therapy Doctoral Program in the School of Health Professions at Presbyterian College (PC-OTD Program). The Capstone/DCE Coordinator is responsible for the organization, implementation, and compliance of the Doctoral Capstone/DCE progression within the PC-OTD Program (ACOTE D-Standards). The Capstone/DCE Coordinator will provide information, resources, and support as students progress from capstone development to completion, dissemination, and commencement. The Capstone/DCE Coordinator will collaborate with faculty, students, and community occupational therapists and other professionals to establish, plan, and execute diverse DCE sites/experiences. The Capstone/DCE Coordinator will ensure the quality, standard and evidence-based focus of student capstone/DCE projects to facilitate professional accomplishment, scholarly dissemination and impact to the profession. As part of this appointment, this faculty member will have 4-8 hours weekly for clinical work and research, to be determined at the discretion of the program director. A review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position has been filled. The duties of the position are as follows: Teaching Teach a course load appropriate to the discipline and position. Plan and organize instruction to maximize student learning objectives. Implement contemporary teaching and learning strategies to communicate subject matter to students. Uphold the expectations of doctoral level work with respect to the program and course learning outcomes. Participate in a culture of assessment of academic integrity, effectiveness and doctoral quality teaching and learning. Modify, as needed, instructional approaches to address student diversity. Learn and use technology to enhance teaching and the educational experience. (internet, Institutional learning system (Brightspace, etc.), distance coursework (tele-medicine /video conferencing as needed), and other interactive technology, as appropriate. Encourage and facilitate the development of communication skills and higher order thinking skills through appropriate assignments. Contribute to the selection and development of instructional materials in accordance with course objectives and the curricular design. Incorporate specific core competencies into specific coursework taught. Develop, update, and post course syllabi in a timely manner in accordance with program policies. Manage classrooms and labs to ensure equipment is maintained and up-to-date. Serve as a mentor and advisor for student’s doctoral projects as assigned. Optimize the learning environment to be conducive to student learning by developing creative, challenging, and evidence-based learning opportunities. Teaching Outcomes Develop and explain methods that fairly measure student progress toward student learning Outcomes and specific course objectives. Evaluate student performance fairly and consistently and return student work promptly to promote maximum learning. Identify students at risk for not meeting performance criteria in order to assist student with engaging the appropriate resources for success. Maintain accurate documentation/records of student progress and submit final grade rosters to Program Director/Administrative staff each semester according to established deadlines. Meet with advisees at least once a semester and maintain accurate documentation any issues, progress, or positives discussed. (note: documentation to be placed in student’s program file) Support of Program Policies and Procedures Post and maintain regular office hours to ensure accessibility for advisement and consultation. Serve as faculty advisor within the current program/college advisement system as assigned. Maintain confidentiality of student information. (i.e. FERPA, HIPPA, ETC) Substitute for other instructors within field or discipline in case of an absence. Exercise stewardship (fiduciary responsibility) of college facilities and materials. Participate in peer evaluation of faculty including observations and reviews as required. Recruit students by supporting and participating in official college recruiting functions, visitation, and hosting of visiting groups as well as participation in interviews, vetting, and decision making of potential admissions to the program. (Student Affairs) Participate in the continuous quality improvement of the OTD program including course/programmatic changes. (Academic Affairs) Facilitate and participate in connections with programs (community, medical, and other organizations) to enhance the OTD program. Participate as a committee member with responsibilities as assigned for national programmatic accreditation. Represent the College/Department in community events relative to the OTD Program. Assist advisees with registration in the Fall, Spring and Summer as needed. Participate in professional activities that contribute to the educational goals of the department college, and its constituents. Perform professional responsibilities in accordance with pertinent goals, missions, and plans of the college and programs. Service Serve on programmatic and university committees as assigned. Actively participate in his/her professional association(s). Serve as university liaison in community and/or professional activities. Participate in PC governance, curriculum planning, and functions to support development and growth of the institution. Participate in PC/OTD Program committees and attends all PC/community functions as assigned. Scholarship Establish and implements an approved scholarship plan/faculty development plan as required by accreditation and PC standards. Stay current with clinical practice and evidence that support content area expertise, professional growth, and professional certification and licensure requirements. Disseminate scholarly work consistent with PC policies and accreditation expectations. Demonstrate commitment to scholarly advancement through research, written artifacts, scholarly inquiry and presentations at state, national and international professional events (minimum one per year). Professionalism Promote professionalism by modeling and encouraging such behaviors inside and outside the classroom setting. Support and exemplify PC mission and core values. Actively engage in interprofessional collaboration activities. Uphold and enforce student and faculty handbook policies and PC policies/procedures. Serve as a role model for faculty members, students, and staff in the department and college. Stay current in subject matter through professional development, involvement in professional organizations, and or attending professional meetings, conferences, or workshops. Demonstrate sensitivity to student needs and circumstances. Maintain awareness/adherence to AOTA Scope of Practice and Code of Ethics Administrative The Capstone/DCE Coordinator manages the capstone/DCE process: Ensure consistency and quality in the creation and completion of the Presbyterian College student Capstone/DCE Project. Assist with the selection of the Research Mentor and Subject Matter Expert (SME) and review credentials as needed with the Program Chair. Maintain a group of doctoral students to advise (~5 per class). Remain current on the timelines, expectations for completion of the Capstone/DCE Project. Have consistent communication with the students and capstone/DCE team members. Mediate any issues that arise related to the completion of the project with students, SME and Faculty. Collaborate with the AFWC to schedule, recruit locations, and address site issues related to the completion of the DCE (implementation or research). Consult and assist the SME with reviews, grading, and collaboration establishing the final grade. Meet with the OTD Program Chair on a regular basis and update the status of the projects Ensure proper Institutional Review Board (IRB) requirements are met Other Other duties as assigned by the Program Director. Minimum Requirements Doctoral Degree/OTD Minimum of three years of experience as an Occupational Therapist Clinical Expertise Teaching responsibilities at the post-secondary education level Scholarship (i.e. Scholarship of application, scholarship of teaching and learning, evidence of progressive Research, Publication) National Board for Certification in Occupational Therapy State OT Licensure Diverse clinical, academic, research background/experience as an Occupational Therapist (minimum 5-years clinical experience). Preferred Requirements Academic/Teaching Experience Student interaction as clinical instructor or coordinator To Apply Interested individuals should provide a cover letter explaining interest in the position, curriculum vitae, unofficial transcripts from all graduate institutions, and three references with contact information. Materials should be sent electronically to the Program Director, Dr. Moni Keen, at [email protected] . Please contact Dr. Moni Keen , Program Director of the OTD Program, at [email protected] if you have any questions about the position. About Presbyterian College Presbyterian College (PC) is a liberal arts college affiliated with the Presbyterian Church USA. The college is committed to an equitable and inclusive learning environment for all students, supports academic freedom, and encourages community involvement. Located in Clinton, South Carolina, PC is approximately 40 minutes from Greenville and Spartanburg, South Carolina and about one hour from Columbia, South Carolina. The region offers abundant opportunity for social and recreational activities, with easy access to Asheville and Charlotte, North Carolina, and Atlanta, Georgia. Presbyterian College is an EEO employer and does not discriminate against any person or group on the basis of age, color, disability, gender, pregnancy, national origin, race, religion, sexual orientation, veteran status or genetic information.  PC is building a culturally diverse faculty and staff committed to working in a multicultural environment and encourages applications from minorities and women. Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources OTD Program Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at [email protected] for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community"
    },
    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/chief-of-campus-police/": {
      "title": "Chief of Campus Police",
      "description": "Chief of Campus Police | Presbyterian College Press Option+1 for screen-reader mode, Option+0 to cancel Accessibility Screen-Reader Guide, Feedback, and Issue Reporting | New window Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Chief of Chief of Campus Police Chief of Campus Police About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Chief of Campus Police Chief of Campus Police Presbyterian College is seeking to hire a Chief of Campus Police. This is a full-time position with benefits and reports to the Vice President for Finance & Administration (VPF&A). The Chief of Police is responsible for the organization, direction and supervision of the College’s Police Department. The Chief of Police administers the program’s activities by formulating polices and providing sound leadership and management. Serve as a resource for law enforcement personnel, faculty, staff and students, and other organizations; coordinates information, resources, and work teams necessary to support a positive, collaborative, productive and harmonious work environment, both within the Police Department and the broader campus community. Position Description The Chief of Police is responsible for all operations of the Campus Police Department and also has oversight of the parking/transportation functions, Clery Act compliance and serves as the Emergency/Risk Manager for the College. As chief law enforcement officer for the College; responsible for the training and development of Campus Police staff. Essential Skills Moderate physical labor may be required on occasion and working outside in adverse weather conditions is common. Must be able to lift up to 50 pounds, walk up and down stairs, and walk or stand for extended periods. Ability to maintain strict confidentiality Computer skills in Microsoft excel, word, PowerPoint and ability to learn other software programs as needed. Ability to handle multiple tasks in a fast-paced environment Ability to work with minimal supervision Good conflict resolution / de-escalation skills Ability to tolerate high levels of stress Good interpersonal and communication skills Ability to plan public events Knowledge of police tactical situations, traffic flow and police training Duties and Responsibilities include but not limited to Performs management functions for the Police Department, such as scheduling, staffing, evaluating, reporting and budgeting Directs and assists with grant writing and administration of grants. Responsible for planning, implementing, administering and coordinating the programs of the Campus Police Department, in addition to providing consultative, technical and administrative direction Responsible for providing programs and training to provide reasonable safety and security for students, faculty, staff and the general public on college property. Training programs should include, but are not limited to, self-defense, sexual assault prevention and general campus safety. Implements programs to promote building and property security. Administers and directs law enforcement functions in all areas relating to the protection of life and property, including police services, crime prevention programs, fire safety and key control. Coordinates efforts with the City of Clinton Police Department and the South Carolina Law Enforcement Division (SLED) Ensures that annual fire drills are conducted in accordance with the South Carolina Fire Code, and serves as the Fire Safety Officer for the College Performs Emergency Management functions, including assisting with development of continuity of operations plans and conducting an annual Emergency Management drill. Ensures Compliance with South Carolina’s Criminal Justice Information Services (CJIS) requirements Responsible for completing the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report as required by The Clery Act , and disseminating the report.  Oversees all aspects of Clery Compliance and serves as the Chair of the Clery Compliance Committee. Administers campus parking and traffic programs and enforces all laws and parking violations. Assist Business Office in collection of fraudulent checks. Provides or assist with security at athletic, cultural and social events on campus and coordinates with the City for off-duty personnel as necessary. Conducts interim and annual performance appraisals for assigned staff. Responsible for administration of the Field Training Program for new officers and oversees the recruitment and hiring of new officers. Manages the development and implementation of departmental goals, objectives, policies and priorities for each assigned service area. Responsible for printing of identification cards for faculty, staff and students. Responsible for the administration of the access control system and setting parameters for access. Arranges for off-duty personnel and outside security/parking services for commencement, homecoming and other high-profile special events. Directs and assists with investigation of major crimes, accidents or other unusual incidents. Selects training, motivates and evaluates assigned personnel; provides or coordinates staff training and career development; works with employees to correct deficiencies, implements discipline and termination procedures. Responsible for ensuring that Timely Warnings (Clery Act) and Emergency Notifications are sent to the campus community as required by police and federal law. Attends and participates in professional group meetings; stays current of new trends and innovations in the field of law enforcement. Ensures compliance with best practices and standards in campus law enforcement. Responds to and resolves difficult and sensitive citizen inquiries and complaints. Perform related duties as required. Implements effective strategies using personnel and technology to prevent crime and solve crimes on campus. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in related field or equivalent combination of related education and required work experience. At least eight (8) years or more of progressive experience in police operations/public safety, including three (3) years of administrative and supervisory experience in police operations/public safety. Must possess Class 1 certification from the South Carolina Criminal Justice Academy or have the ability to obtain his or her South Carolina Justice Academy certification within one year. Required Education, Licensure, Certification, Registration, or Other Requirements Valid South Carolina Driver’s license Be at least twenty-one (21) years of age (SLED REQUIREMENT) Far visual acuity of at least 20/30 in each eye with or without corrective lenses; Vision capable of distinguishing basic color groups against a favorable background; The ability to obtain SLED Certification or currently have obtained SLED Certification as a Level 1 law enforcement officer. Ability to maintain the requirements set forth in Article 9, Chapter 73, S.C. Code of Regulations (73-400 to 73-422). South Carolina Law Enforcement Division; As a part of the pre-employment process, the selected candidate must be willing to consent to and pass with satisfactory results, an investigative consumer report, a SLED background check, a pre-employment drug screen, a driver’s license check, a medical exam and psychological exam. Must reside within 35 miles of Clinton, SC, or be willing to relocate within 35 miles of Clinton, SC Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree or higher in related field. At least 10 years’ experience in law enforcement At least 4 years’ experience as a law enforcement supervisor, including 2 years of Command Level experience Completion of FBI National Academy, Southern Police Institute, Administrative Officer’s Management Program or equivalent Command Training Experience working in a campus law enforcement setting To Apply Send a cover letter, résumé and list of three references including contact information to Presbyterian College Office of Human Resources, 503 S. Broad St., Clinton, SC 29325 or email materials to hr@presby.edu About Presbyterian College Presbyterian College (PC) is a liberal arts college affiliated with the Presbyterian Church USA. The college is committed to an equitable and inclusive learning environment for all students, supports academic freedom, and encourages community involvement.  Located in Clinton, South Carolina, PC is approximately 40 minutes from Greenville and Spartanburg, South Carolina and about one hour from Columbia, South Carolina. The region offers abundant opportunity for social and recreational activities, with easy access to Asheville and Charlotte, North Carolina, and Atlanta, Georgia. The College seeks to hire the most qualified candidate and does not discriminate against any legally protected class. Presbyterian College is an EEO employer and does not discriminate against any person or group on the basis of age, color, disability, gender, pregnancy, national origin, race, religion, sexual orientation, veteran status or genetic information.  PC is building a culturally diverse faculty and staff committed to working in a multicultural environment and encourages applications from minorities and women. Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources Campus Safety Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at accommodations@presby.edu for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community English Spanish en"
